---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My Testimony - Caleb Michael McGennis"
  description="Caleb Michael McGennis shares his testimony: growing up in the faith, losing his father at fifteen, and the twenty-year answer to a fifth grader's prayer for wisdom."
  type="article"
  author="Caleb Michael McGennis"
  image="/images/social/testimony-caleb-og-card.jpg"
  hideNewsletterPopup={true}
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="bg-soho-white py-20 md:py-32 px-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
      <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-mcgennis-gold mb-6">Testimony</p>
      <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-display font-bold mb-8 text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">
        My Testimony
      </h1>
      <div class="h-1 w-24 bg-brass-horizontal rounded-full shadow-sm mx-auto mb-8"></div>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 font-light">
        Caleb Michael McGennis
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Testimony -->
  <article class="py-16 md:py-24 px-4">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl">

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Foundation</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I grew up a Christian my whole life. That's the honest starting place. There was never a season where God was unfamiliar to me &mdash; He was in the house before I could tell you who He was.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        My mom taught me Bible verses before I was old enough to understand what they meant, and she made up songs out of them so they would stick. Those songs are still in me. I can still hear some of them. She wasn't trying to make me a theologian; she was trying to put something in me that would hold. It held.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Up until about the fifth grade, I remember having a real prayer life. Not a performance, not a bedtime routine &mdash; an actual back-and-forth. And what I remember praying for most was wisdom.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-mcgennis-gold pl-6 my-10">
        <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl text-gray-700 font-light italic leading-relaxed">
          What good is having knowledge if I don't know what to do with it?
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That's a strange thing for a fifth grader to fixate on. But even then, the logic made sense to me. I didn't have language for it at the time, but I was already asking God to be involved in how I lived &mdash; not just in what I knew.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Decision</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Around fifth or sixth grade, I was in church one Sunday when they asked us to say the sinner's prayer. My father walked me through it. And I want to be honest about how that went, because it matters to the rest of the story: he was strict enough about it that any kid in that room would have said the words.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        So in the weeks that followed, my mom felt the need to follow up. She wanted to make sure the decision I made was actually mine and not just something I'd been marched through.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I'm glad she did. Because as we had those conversations, I found out I wholeheartedly understood what I had decided. I believed in God. I knew I was a sinner. I knew He sent His Son to die on the cross for my sins.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        But more than the doctrine, I knew what I wanted. What I wanted more than anything was a relationship with God. I wanted to talk to Him. To be with Him. To laugh with Him and cry with Him. That's what I was saying yes to. Not a formula &mdash; a person.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Over the years that followed, my walk with Christ wasn't always at the front of my priorities. It was there. It just wasn't first. That stayed true until I was fifteen.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Morning Everything Changed</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I was sitting in my room when my grandma showed up at the house. I could tell immediately &mdash; by the way she was acting, and by the way my mom was acting &mdash; that something was wrong.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        My dad, Brian, had been on and off drugs for basically our whole lives. There would be weeks at a time when he was gone. Sometimes months at a rehab facility. So it wasn't a surprise that he wasn't home that morning. What was a surprise was that he was in the hospital.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        When we got to the hospital, my dad lay there in a coma. The doctors explained what had actually happened &mdash; an overdose, and he had asphyxiated.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        But that isn't the version that left the room. We settled on "heart attack" for everyone else. Easier to explain. Easier to accept.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        My sisters and I got the softened version too. The full truth of that night &mdash; where he'd been, what he'd taken, how long he was on that porch alone &mdash; we learned much, much later.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        My dad had gone to his dealer the night before, and the dealer was out of his usual vice. So my dad tried something else the dealer had. He went out to the porch while he gave in to it, and he started to convulse. He fell face first out of his chair. While he was on the ground, his body reacted badly to the drugs and he began to vomit.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        No one was around. My dad suffocated on his own vomit.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        He was the person I looked up to more than anyone. And he was gone before I understood how much of me came from him.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What It Cost</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        When my dad passed, my relationship with my mom kind of passed with him. I looked up to my dad more than anyone. I showed love and received love the way he did. My mom is the opposite of that, and losing the one made the distance from the other feel even wider than it already was.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        It was a hard season. I went looking for that love in relationships. I was still plugged into churches along the way, but they weren't my priority.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Here's the part I want to say clearly, because I think people expect the other answer: I wasn't frustrated with God for taking my dad. I was sad with God that my dad was gone.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-mcgennis-navy pl-6 my-10">
        <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl text-gray-700 font-light italic leading-relaxed">
          I never once felt like He was the one who did this to me. I felt like He was the one sitting in it with me.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That distinction is probably the only reason I'm still here writing this.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        As the years went on, I kept trying to find where I fit. I succeeded at a lot of things. None of it felt right. I've written the longer version of that season in <a href="/blog/entrepreneur-ai/theres-no-place-like-home" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">There's No Place Like Home</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Chicago, and a Man Named Chris</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Nothing felt right until I was offered an internship at the Missouri Baptist Convention. I was in a small group at the time led by Mitch Canote, and he was the one who extended the offer. Next thing I knew, I was off to Chicago the following week.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        That trip is where I was impacted the most. And it's where I met Chris.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Chris was the first man to believe in me since my dad passed away. I don't say that lightly, and I don't think he fully knew what he was stepping into when he did it.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Over the next five or six years, Chris and Mitch showed me how to serve God from a ministry support role. I had always pictured myself as the support guy anyway &mdash; I've got a "what can I do to help" personality. They didn't try to make me into something I'm not. They showed me that the way God wired me was useful to Him.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Through the internship, I was offered a contracting position to test my abilities. Everything they threw on my plate, I ate. Anything I could help with, I wanted to help with.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">"When Are You Going to Hire My Son?"</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The Missouri Baptist Convention sits on East High Street, and every so often we'd walk downtown for lunch. On one beautiful day, we happened to run into my mom.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Without so much as a hi &mdash; no greeting, no warm-up &mdash; she looked straight at my boss and said, "When are you going to hire my son?"
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Chris was so taken off guard he just kind of laughed.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        I had been working on contract for about a year by then, and my mom had been asking me that same question the entire time. Every time, I'd just tell her soon.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Well &mdash; that soon had come. Not long after, Chris decided it didn't make sense not to hire me.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What Grew</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Working at the convention put me around strong Christian men who came to mean the world to me. Everything they did, I wanted to do. Anything they needed help with, I wanted to help with.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        But when I look back at those years, the thing that grew the most wasn't my skill set. It was my relationship with God. More than my abilities, more than my career, more than anything &mdash; that's what got built in that season.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Which is the whole thing, really. It's what the fifth grader was praying for when he asked for wisdom instead of knowledge. It's what the sixth grader meant when he said he wanted to talk with God, be with Him, laugh with Him and cry with Him. It took about twenty years and the worst morning of my life, but He answered exactly what I asked for.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The story of how that led back into ministry &mdash; and how God moved the mountain to get me to Concord &mdash; is in <a href="/blog/entrepreneur-ai/the-mountain-mover" class="text-mcgennis-navy hover:text-mcgennis-gold transition-colors underline">The Mountain Mover</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-16 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Where I Am Now</h2>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Now I'm just walking through life with Christ.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-50 via-white to-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 md:p-12 my-12 text-center">
        <p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl text-gray-800 font-display font-bold leading-relaxed tracking-tight mb-4">
          My money &mdash; His.<br />
          My time &mdash; His.<br />
          My body &mdash; His.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 font-light">
          If it comes into my account, it isn't mine. It's His.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

